  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nFair weather cumulus clouds are present across the area as high\npressure dominates the weather pattern. Temperatures will\nstruggle to make it our of the 70s this afternoon as morning fog\nand clouds allowed surface heating to get going a little later\nthan normal, lowering the afternoon temperature potential. High\npressure continues tonight with another round of fog expected\nacross our valleys. Lows should be in the low to mid 50s for\nmuch of the region.\n\nOn Wednesday, the upper level trough will dig south into the\neastern US with 2 disturbances propagating through the pattern;\none moving over the Great Lakes and turning NE into Canada, and \nthe other across the Ohio River Valley into the Mid-Atlantic.\nThe first disturbance will bring a chance for rain showers with\nisolated thunder starting in the late morning hours. The energy\nwith this system looks to quickly move off to the NE and into\nCanada, so more widespread showers across WNY in the morning\nshould taper down to scattered showers across CNY in the\nafternoon. A weak boundary pushed into the area from the NW \nshould allow for some afternoon showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to develop in the afternoon across CNY. A jet\nstreak will move overhead during the evening and overnight\nhours, allowing for some lingering showers to be possible.\nThe 2nd disturbance is progged to move through the Ohio River\nValley and into the Mid-Atlantic by Wednesday morning. A weak\nsurface low should develop by this time generating scattered \nrain showers. The track of the low remains somewhat uncertain as\na surface high will build into the Great Lakes and WNY as this\ndisturbance moves into the Mid-Atlantic. The strength and speed\nof the high will help determine the track and speed of the low,\nwhich is currently forecast to track somewhere between NEPA and\nSNJ Thursday morning or Thursday afternoon. Rain should be light\nwith up to 0.25in possible. High pressure should be fully in\ncontrol by late afternoon on Thursday. \n\nTemps on Wednesday should be near normal with highs in the upper\n70s to mid 80s. Thursday will see a few degrees cooler with\nhighs in the 70s.\n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe upper level trough is forecast to deepen across the eastern\nUS on Saturday and slightly tilt negatively, generating a low\nover the Mid-Atlantic and another over the Great Lakes. The \naforementioned high pressure system will slowly push NE as \nSaturday progresses, but its influence may be strong enough to \nkeep rain at bay for most of the day on Saturday. If it moves \nout quicker and isn't as strong, rain showers should push into \nthe area on Saturday. If it remains strong, rain should move\ninto the area Sunday. The track and speed of the low pressure\nsystems remains uncertain so confidence in rainfall amounts are\nlow, but we do expected rain showers in the area. The trough\naxis should push east of the area sometime Monday, drying us out\nand allowing another high pressure system to move into the area\nfrom the NW. High pressure should remain in place into mid-week. \nTemperatures should be slightly below normal, with highs in the\n70s and lows in the 50s.",
